X:2
T:Adieu Sweet Lovely Nancy
M:4/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=120
N:In honor and memory of Nancy Coe Bailey, longtime host of the Dupont Music Circle
K:G
%
z2 z4 GA | "G" B2 B2 B2 AB | "C" c4 "G" d2 dc | BA G2 "D" A2 A2 | "G" G6 GA |
w: A_dieu, sweet love-ly_ Nan-cy, ten_ thou_sand times a-dieu. I am
%
B2 BB B2 AB | "C" c2 c2 "G" d2 dc | BA G2 "D" A2 A2 | "G" G6 d2 |
w: go-ing a-cross the_ o-cean wide to_ seek_ for some-thing new. Come
%
"C" e2 d2 "G" d2 g2 | d2 B2 c2 d2 | "C" e2 d2 "G" BA G2 | "D" A6 GA |
w: change a ring with me, dear girl, come change a ring_ with me. For_
%
"G" B2 B2 B2 AB | "C" cc cd "G" d2 dc | BA G2 "D" A2 A2 | "G" G6 z2 |]
w: it will be a_ to-ken of true love while_ I_ am on the sea.
%
%%leftmargin 1.5in
%%rightmargin 1.5in
%
W: 2. And when I'm far upon the sea you'll know not where I am.
W: Kind letters I will write to you from every foreign land.
W: The secrets of your heart, dear girl, are the best of my good will.
W: So let your body be where it might, my heart will be with you still.
W:
W: 3. There's a heavy storm arising, see how it gathers round.
W: While we poor souls on the ocean wide are fighting for the crown.
W: There's nothing to protect us, love, or keep us from the cold.
W: On the ocean wide, where we must fight like jolly seamen bold.
W:
W: 4. There's tinkers, tailors, shoemakers, lie snoring fast asleep.
W: While we poor souls on the ocean wide are ploughing through the deep.
W: Our officers commanded us, and them we must obey.
W: Expecting every moment for to get cast away.
W:
W: 5. But when the wars are over, there'll be peace on every shore.
W: We'll return to our wives and our families, and the girls that we adore.
W: We'll drink our liquor merrily, and spend our money free.
W: And when the money is all gone, we'll boldly go to sea.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_5e6a3254.abc

X:3
T:Ae Fond Kiss
C:Words by Robert Burns
L:1/4
M:3/4
Q:1/4=120
K:C
%
"C" E2 E | G2 A | "Am" C2 C | A, C2 |
w: Ae fond kiss, and then we se-ver.
"F" A2 A | c2 A | "C" G2 D | E G2 |
w: Ae fare-weel, a-las, for-ev-er!
"F" F2 A | c2 C | "Am" C2 C | A, C2 |
w: Deep in heart-wrung tears I'll pledge thee.
"C" E2 D | E2 A | "Em" G3- | G2 E | "Am" D E2- | E3 |]
w: War-ring sighs and groans_ I'll wage thee!_
%
%%leftmargin 2.5in
%%rightmargin 2.5in
%
W:2. Who shall say that Fortune grieves him
W:While the star of hope she leaves him?
W:Me, nae cheerfu' twinkle lights me,
W:Dark despair around benights me.
W:
W:3. I'll ne'er blame my partial fancy;
W:Naething could resist my Nancy;
W:But to see her was to love her,
W:Love but her, and love for ever.
W:
W:4. Had we never loved sae kindly,
W:Had we never loved sae blindly,
W:Never met, or never parted,
W:We had ne'er been broken-hearted.
W:
W:5. Fare thee weel, thou first and fairest!
W:Fare thee weel, thou best and dearest!
W:Thine be ilka joy and treasure,
W:Peace, enjoyment, love, and pleasure!
W:
W:6. Ae fond kiss, and then we sever!
W:Ae fareweel, alas, for ever!
W:Deep in heart-wrung tears I'll pledge thee,
W:Warring sighs and groans I'll wage thee!
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_0adc5006.abc

