%%leftmargin 1.5in
%%rightmargin 1.5in
W: 1. All things are quite silent, each mortal at rest,
W: When me and my true love got snug in one nest,
W: When a bold set of ruffians they entered our cave,
W: And they forced my dear jewel to plough the salt wave.
W:
W: 2. I begged hard for my sailor as I begged for my life.
W: They'd not listen to me although a fond wife,
W: Saying: "The King he want's sailors, to the sea he must go."
W: And they left me lamenting in sorrow and woe.
W:
W: 3. Through green ﬁelds and meadows we ofttimes did walk,
W: And sweet conversation of love we have talked,
W: With the birds in the woodland so sweetly did sing,
W: And the young thrushes' voices made the valleys to ring.
W:
W: 4. Although my love's gone I will not be cast down.
W: Who knows but my sailor may once more return?
W: And will make me amends for all trouble and strife,
W: And my true love and I might live happy for life.
%
