%
W: 1. When I was apprenticed in London
W: I went to see my dear
W: The candles all were burning
W: The moon shone bright and clear.
W: I knocked upon her window
W: To ease her of her pain
W: She rose to let me in
W: Then she barred the door again.
W:
W: 2. I like your well behaviour
W: And this I often say
W: I cannot rest contented
W: When I am far away.
W: The roads they are so muddy
W: We cannot walk about
W: So roll me in your arms my love
W: And blow the candles out.
W:
W: 3. Your father and your mother
W: In yonder room do lie
W: A-hugging one another
W: So why not you and I?
W: A-hugging one another
W: Without a fear or doubt
W: So roll me in your arms my love
W: And blow the candles out.
W:
W: 4. I prithee speak more softly
W: Of what we have to do
W: Lest that our noise of talking
W: Should make our pleasure rue.
W: The streets they are so nigh, love
W: The people walk about
W: They may peep in and spy, love
W: So blow the candles out.
W:
W: 5. And if we prove successful, love
W: Please name it after me.
W: Treat it neat and kiss it sweet
W: And daff it on your knee.
W: When my three years are over
W: My time it will be out
W: And I will pay my debt to you
W: By blowing the candles out.
%
