"C" A G3 E2 | "F" F2 "C" E2 "Dm" D2 | "F" C4 CD | "C" E G3GF | E2 "F" C2 z C | "C" EG "F"  F E2 C | "C" C6 |]
w: past us, right hap-py were we. It was lit-tle she_ thought that a sol--dier_ I'd be.
%
W: 1. Down by yon green bushes near Calder's clear stream
W: Where me and my Annie so often have been
W: When the hours that flew past us, right happy were we
W: It was little she thought that a soldier I'd be.
W:
W: 2. But it's farewell to Annie and I must away
W: For the King he needs soldiers and I must obey
W: But if providence prove kind love until I return
W: I will wed with my Annie near Calder's clear burn.
W:
W: 3. On the fourteenth of August our regiment was lost
W: And a ball from the enemy our lines came across
W: O it struck me in the temple and the blood trickled down
W: I reeled and I staggered and I fell to the ground
W:
W: 4. Come here, says our captain, come here with good speed
W: For I fear by this bullet young Dinsmore lies dead
W: Two men with a stretcher did quickly prepare
W: And they carried me away to a hospital there.
W:
W: 5. Cold water and brandy they poured out so free
W: They turned me all over my wounds for to see
W: But if I had my Annie to bind up my wounds
W: One kiss from her sweet lips would soon deaden the stoun.
W:
W: 6. And it's when I am weary and think on lang syne
W: When I was a miner and wrought in the mine
W: O the tears they do trickle and down they do fall
W: Like the roses that bloom around bonny Woodhall.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_5e624a6c.abc

