W: 2. And what are the joys of a poor married man? Why, boozin', bloody well boozin'.
W: And what is he doing whenever he can? Why, boozin', bloody well boozin'.
W: He comes home at night and he gives his wife all.
W: Then goes out shopping, makes many a call.
W: But what brings him home hanging onto the wall?
W: Why, boozin', bloody well boozin'.
W:
W: [Chorus:]
W: Boozin', boozin', just you and I. Boozin', boozin', when we are dry.
W: Some do it openly, some on the sly. And we all are bloody well boozin'.
W:
W: 3. And what does the Salvation Army run down? Why, boozin', bloody well boozin'.
W: And what are they damning in every town? Why, boozin', bloody well boozin'.
W: They stand on street corners, they rant and they shout.
W: They shout about things they know nothing about.
W: But what are they doing when the lights are all out?
W: Why, boozin', bloody well boozin'.
W: [Chorus:]
%
