w: If I had a- no- ther pen_\-ny|I would have a- no- ther gill_  and|I would make the pi- per play_  the|bon- ny lass of By- ker Hill.|
%
W: 1. If I had another penny
W: I would have another gill
W: And I would make the piper play
W: The Bonny Lass of Byker Hill
W:
W: Chorus:
W: Byker Hill and Walker Shore, my lads
W: Collier lads for ever more, my boys
W: Byker Hill and Walker Shore, my lads
W: Collier lads for ever more
W:
W: 2. Me Ginny she sits over late up
W: Me Ginny she sits over late up
W: Me Ginny she sits over late up
W: Between the pint pot and the cup
W:
W: 3. It’s down the pits we’ll go my laddies
W: It’s down the pits we’ll go my marrers
W: Well try our wills and use our skill
W: To cut them ridges down below
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 4. Me Ginny she is never near
W: Me Ginny she is never near
W: And when I call out, “Where’s my supper?”
W: She orders up another pint of beer
W:
W: 5. When first I come into the dirt
W: I had no trousers nor pitshirt
W: And now I’m getting two or three
W: Walker Pit done well by me
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 6. Hey Ginny come home to your little baby
W: Hey Ginny come home to your little baby
W: Hey Ginny come home to your little baby
W: With a pint of beer all under your arm
W:
W: 7. The poor coal cutter gets two shillings
W: The deputy get half a crown
W: And the overman gets five and sixpence
W: That’s just for riding up and down
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 8. Geordie Johnson had a pig
W: And he hit it with a shovel and it danced a jig
W: All the way to Byker Hill
W: He danced the Elsie Marley
W: (Chorus)
