X:58
T:Dancing at Whitsun
C:Lyrics: John Austin Marshall
M:3/4
L:1/8
K:C
%recorded by Bok, Trickett, Muir. Also Jean Redpath, and Tim Hart and Maddy Prior on Summer Solstice.
%
G2 | "C" c2 c2 d2 | c2 G3 c | "Dm" d2 e2 f2 | "C" g4 (gf) | e3 d c2 |
w: It's fif-ty long spring times since she was a bride, but_ still you can
"Dm" (de) f3 e | d2 c2 A2 | "G" G4 ef | "C" g2 e2 g2 | "Dm" f2 d3 f | "C" (ed) c2 d2 |
w: see_ her at each Whit-sun-tide. In a dress of white lin-en with rib-_ bons of
"G" G4 (AB) | "Am" c3 d e2 | "Dm" fe d2 c2 | "G" B2 "C" c4- | c4 z2 |]
w: green, as_ green as her mem-o-ries of lov-ing._
%
W: 1. It's fifty long springtimes since she was a bride,
W: But still you may see her at each Whitsuntide.
W: In a dress of white linen with ribbons of green,
W: As green as her memories of loving.
W:
W: 2. The feet that were nimble tread carefully now,
W: As gentle a measure as age will allow,
W: Through groves of white blossoms, by fields of young corn,
W: Where once she was pledged to her true-love.
W:
W: 3. The fields they stand empty, the hedges grow free,
W: No young men to turn them or pastures go see,
W: They have gone where the forest of oak trees before
W: Had gone to be wasted in battle.
W:
W: 4. Down from the green farmlands and from their loved ones
W: Marched husbands and brothers and fathers and sons.
W: There's a fine roll of honor where the Maypole once stood,
W: And the ladies go dancing at Whitsun.
W:
W: 5. There's a straight row of houses in these latter days
W: All covering the downs where the sheep used to graze.
W: There's a field of red poppies and a wreath from the Queen,
W: But the ladies remember at Whitsun,
W: And the ladies go dancing at Whitsun.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_095abaca.abc

