W: 1. Poor old Dicey Riley she has taken to the sup.
W: Poor old Dicey Riley she will never give it up.
W: It's off each morning to the hock,
W: And then she's in for another little drop.
W: But the heart of the rowl is Dicey Riley.
W:
W: 2. She walks along Fitzgibbon Street with an independent air.
W: And then it's down to Summerhill while all the people stare.
W: She says "It's nearly half past one, so I'll nip in for another little one."
W: But the heart of the rowl is Dicey Riley.
W:
W: 3. At two pubs close and out she goes as happy as a lark.
W: She'll find a bench to sleep it off down in St. Patrick's Park.
W: She'll wake at five feeling in the pink and say, "Tis time for another little drink."
W: But the heart of the rowl is Dicey Riley.
W:
W: 4. She owns a little sweetshop at the corner of the street.
W: And every evening after school I go to wash her feet.
W: She leaves me there to mind the shop and she nips in for another little drop.
W: But the heart of the rowl is Dicey Riley.
W:
W: 5. Oh they carry her home at twelve o'clock as they do every night.
W: Bring her inside, put her on the bed and then turn out the light.
W: Next morning she'll get out of bed, and look for a cure for her head.
W: But the heart of the rowl is Dicey Reilly.
W:
W: 6. Ah poor old Dicey Riley she has taken to the sup.
W: Poor old Dicey Riley she will never give it up.
W: It's off each morning to the hock,
W: And then she's in for another little drop.
W: But the heart of the rowl is Dicey Riley.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_f280af39.abc

