w: There was a fair maid of Is_ling_ton as_ I've_ heard_ ma-ny tell, And
"D" D2 "Dsus4" G2 "D" FF D2 | FG AB "C" c2 dc | "D" AG AG FF "C" DC | "D" D4 z2  AB |
w: she was go-ing to Lon_don_ town her_ pears_ and_ ap-ples to_ sell. As_
"Am" c2 A2 "D" dd AG | FG AB "C" c2 dc | "D" AG FG A2 D2 | "C" C4 z2 A,2 |
w: she was go-ing a_long_ the_ road a_ vint_ner_ did she spy. "And
"D" D2 "G" GG "D" F2 D2 | FG AB "C" c2 dc | "D" AG AG F2 "C" DC | "D" D6 z2 |]
w: what shall I give, fair maid,”_ says_ he, “one_ night_ with_ you to_ lie?”
%
W: 1. There was a fair maid of Islington as I've heard many tell,
W: And she was going to London town her pears and apples to sell.
W: As she was going along the road a vintner did she spy.
W: “And what shall I give, fair maid,” says he, “one night with you to lie?”
W:
W: 2. “If you would lay with me one night you must give to me five pounds.”
W: “A match, a match,” the vintner said, “so let this go round.”
W: When he had lain with her all night her money she did crave,
W: “No, oh no,” the vintner says, “the devil a penny you'll have!”
W:
W: 3. This maid she made no more ado but to the justice went.
W: “This vintner hired a cellar of me and he will not pay the rent.”
W: So straight the justice for him sent and asked him the reason why
W: That he would pay the maid no rent to which he did reply:
W:
W: 4. “Although I hired a cellar of her and the possession was mine
W: I never put anything into it but one small pipe of wine.”
W: This maid she being ripe of wit she straight replied again,
W: “There lay two butts at the cellar door why didn't you roll them in?”
W:
W: 5. The justice told the vintner plain if he a tenant be
W: He must epect to pay the rent; he could not sit rent free.
W: And when the maid her money got she put it all in her purse
W: And clapped her hand on the cellar door and said it was none the worse.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_06980d90.abc

