W: 1. As I was a-walking one Midsummer’s morning,
W: The fields and the meadows were pleasant and gay.
W: 'Twas there I espied a most beautiful damsel,
W: As she was a-tripping along the highway.
W:
W: 2. I stepped up to her and wished her Good Morning!
W: And where are you going so soon in the morn?
W: She answered, Kind Sir, I'm a-going to Windsor,
W: To that pleasant place where I was born,
W:
W: 3. I says, Fair maid, and shall I go with you,
W: All for to bear you sweet company,
W: She turned herself round and smiling so sweetly,
W: Kind Sir, you may do just as you please.
W:
W: 4. We had not been walking past a mile together,
W: Before that we better acquainted became.
W: I says, Fair Girl, come sit you down by me,
W: And I will show you a sweet, pleasant game.
W:
W: 5. She says, Kind Sir, I'm not given to gaming,
W: But nevertheless I'm willing to learn,
W: But the game that we play it must be "All Fours",
W: And then I will hold you three to one.
W:
W: 6. She cut the cards and 'twas my turn to deal them,
W: I dealt myself one trump, 'twas only poor Jack,
W: And she had the Ace and the Deuce for to beat me,
W: And they are the very best cards in the pack.
W:
W: 7. She played off her Ace and she took the Jack from me,
W: Which made her "High, Low, Jack and the Game",
W: She says, Kind Sir, I've fairly a-beat you,
W: Or else we will play the game over again.
W:
W: 8. I picked up my hat and I wished her "Good Morning!"
W: And left her "High, Low, Jack and the Game".
W: I says, Fair Girl, I'll be this way tomorrow,
W: And then we will play the game over again.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_5be70248.abc

