w:One bright sum-mer day when the hea-ther was bloom-ing, and the
%
"Am" c d g | "C" e2 d/2B/2 | "Em" A G B | "G" d2 d |
w:hol-low hills hummed with the hon-ey lade bee, I
%
"C" e g f | e "G" d> B | "Am" A G E | "Dsus4" G "D" A3/2 A/4A/4 |
w:met a fair maid-en as home-ward I ram-bled. She was
%
"G" B c d | "Em" e2 E/2E/2 | "Am" A3/2 G/2"G" B | "Am" A3 |]
w:herd-ing her sheep on the hills of Glen-shee.
%
%
%%leftmargin 2.00in
%%rightmargin 2.00in
%
W:1. One bright summer day when the heather was blooming,
W:and the hollow hills hummed with the honey lade bee,
W:I met a fair maiden as homeward I rambled.
W:She was herding her sheep on the hills of Glenshee.
W:
W:2. I stood all amazed, said I, "Bonnie Lassie,
W:If you will but go into Jamestown with me,
W:A bright carriage you'll have for to ride at your leisure.
W:I'll make you my bride, oh, lass of Glenshee."
W:
W:3. "Oh, I don't care at all for your horses and carriages,
W:And I don't care at all for your bold quality.
W:I'd rather be here in my own coun-ter-ee, sir,
W:A-herding my sheep on the hills of Glenshee."
W:
W:4. Oh the lark may forget her sweet song in the morning,
W:And the rivers forget to run down to the sea,
W:But never will I while my senses do govern,
W:Forget the fine lass I met in Glenshee.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_61bc2a41.abc

