X:114
T:I'm a Freeborn Man
C:By Ewan McColl
L:1/4
M:4/4
I:linebreak $
K:D
%
"D" D E A d |[M:6/4] A2 F3/2 D/ G A |[M:4/4] "Em" G2 E2 |$ z2 F G | "A" A3/2 A/ c d | e f e c | "D" d2 A2 |$
w: I'm a free\- born|man of the trav\- eling|peo\- ple,|Got no|fixed a\- bode, with|no\- mads I am|num\- bered.|
z2 c d |[M:6/4] "A" e f e2 d3/2 c/ | "D" d A "G" B2 "D" A3/2 F/ |$ "G" G B "D" A A "C" G/ E3/2 | =C2 "D" D2 z2 |[M:4/4] z4 |]
w: Coun\- try|lanes and high\- ways were|al\- ways my ways, I|nev\- er fan\- cied be\- ing|lum\- bered.|
%
W: 1. I’m a freeborn man of the travelling people,
W: Got no fied abode, with nomads I am numbered.
W: Country lanes and by-ways were always my ways,
W: I never fancied being lumbered.
W:
W: 2. O, we knew the woods and the resting places,
W: And the small birds sang when winter-time was over;
W: Then we’d pack our load and be on the road,
W: They were good old times for a rover.
W:
W: 3. There was open ground where a man could linger,
W: Stay a week or two, for time was not your master.
W: Then away you’d jog with your horse and dog,
W: Nice and easy, no need to go faster.
W:
W: 4. Now and then you’d meet up with other Travellers,
W: Hear the news or else swap family information;
W: At the country fairs, you’d be meeting there
W: All the people of the travelling nation.
W:
W: 5. O, I’ve known life hard and I’ve known it easy,
W: And I’ve cursed the times when winter days were dawning;
W: But I’ve danced and sang through the whole night long,
W: Seen the summer sun rise in the morning.
W:
W: 6. All you freeborn men of the travelling people,
W: Every tinker, rolling stone and gypsy rover:
W: Winds of change are blowing, old ways are going,
W: Your travelling days will soon be over.
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_ceed86b8.abc

