w: I'm a ro-ver, and sel-dom so-ber: I'm a ro_ver of high_ de-gree. It's when I'm
B2 dc/2B/2 AB | G2 D2 GA | B2 B/2A/2G A>G | G4 z2 |]
w: drink-ing I'm_ al-ways think-ing How to gain my_ love's com-pa-ny.
%
%%leftmargin .5in
%
W: 1. Though the nicht be as dark as dungeon,
W: Nae a star tae be seen above.
W: I will be guided withoot a stumble
W: To the arms of my ain true love.
W:
W: Chorus:
W: I'm a rover, and seldom sober:
W: I'm a rover of high degree.
W: It's when I'm drinking I'm always thinking
W: How to gain my love's company.
W:
W: 2. He's steppit up tae her bedroom windae.
W: Kneelin' gently all on a stane.
W: He's rappit all on her bedroom windae;
W: "Darlin' dear, do ye lie alane?"
W:
W: 3. She's raised her head on her snow-white pillow,
W: Wi' her arms aboot her breast.
W: "Wha' is that at my bedroom windae,
W: Disturbin' me at my long night's rest?"
W:
W: 4. "It's only me, your ain true lover.
W: Open the door and you let me in:
W: For I have come on a long, long journey,
W: And I'm near drenchit tae the skin.
W:
W: 5. She's opened the door wi' the greatest pleasure,
W: Opened the door and she's let him in.
W: They both shook hands and embraced each other.
W: 'Til the morning they lay as one.
W:
W: 6. The cocks were crowing, the birds were whistling,
W: The burn ran free abane the brae.
W: "Remember, lass, I'm a plowman laddie.
W: And the farmer I must obey."
W:
W: 7. "Noo me lass, I must gang and leave thee,
W: Though the hills they are high above.
W: I will climb them with greater pleasure
W: Since I've been in the arms of me love.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_9f57dd79.abc

