w: I was a young man, I was a rov-er, Noth-ing would sa-tis-fy me but a wife.
A A D D/D/ | "Am" E E/D/ C A, | C C/D/ E A/A/ | "C" G E "Dm" D2- | D4 |]
w: Soon as I reached the age of_ twen-ty Wear-y was I of a sin-gle life._
%
W: 2. The very first year my wife I married,
W: Out of her company I could not stay.
W: Her voice was sweet as the lark or the linnet
W: Or the nightingale at the break of day.
W:
W: 3. Now she’s fairly altered her meaning,
W: Now she’s fairly changed her tune.
W: Nothing but scolding comes from her mouth
W: So the poor man’s labour’s never done.
W:
W: 4. The very first year that we were married
W: Scarce could I get one half hour’s sleep.
W: With her two heels she rubbed my shins,
W: Cries, “Husband dear, put down your feet.”
W:
W: 5. The baby cried, she bitterly scolded,
W: Down to the door I was forced for to run.
W: Without trousers, wig or a waistcoat,
W: The poor man’s labour’s never done.
W:
W: 6. I went up to the top of the hill
W: For to view my sheep that had all gone astray.
W: When I came back she was lying in her bed
W: At twelve o’clock on a winter’s day.
W:
W: 7. When I came back both wet and weary,
W: Weary and wet, now where could I run?
W: She was lying in her bed, the fire up beside her,
W: She said, “Young man, is the kettle on?”
W:
W: 8. I’ll go home to my aged mother,
W: She’ll be sitting all alone;
W: Says there’s plenty young women to be had
W: Why should I be tied to one?
W:
W: 9. All young men that is to marry
W: Though they’ll grieve you ever more,
W: Death o Death, come take my wife
W: And then my sorrows will be o’er.
%
