W:2. They've let him lie for a very long time
W:Till the rains from heaven did fall
W:And little Sir John sprung up his head
W:And so amazed them all
W:They've let him stand till midsummer's day
W:Till he looked both pale and wan
W:And little Sir John's grown a long, long beard
W:And so become a man.
W:
W:3. They've hired men with the scythes so sharp
W:To cut him off at the knee
W:They've rolled him and tied him by the waist
W:Serving him most barbarously
W:They've hired men with the sharp pitchforks
W:Who pricked him to the heart
W:And the loader he has served him worse than that
W:For he's bound him to the cart.
W:
W:4. They've wheeled him around and around the field
W:Till they came unto a barn
W:And there they made a solemn oath
W:On poor John Barleycorn
W:They've hired men with the crab-tree sticks
W:To cut him skin from bone
W:And the miller he has served him worse than that
W:For he's ground him between two stones.
W:
W:5. And little Sir John and the nut-brown bowl
W:And he's brandy in the glass
W:And little Sir John and the nut-brown bowl
W:Proved the strongest man at last
W:The huntsman, he can't hunt the fox
W:Nor so loudly to blow his horn
W:And the tinker he can't mend kettle nor pot
W:Without a little Barleycorn.
