W: 2. "Good morning to you, my pretty fair maid."
W: "Twice good morning, sir.", she said.
W: He tipped her the wink, and she rolled her dark eye,
W: Says he to himself, "I'll be there by and by."
W:
W: 3. "Oh, don't you think these pooks of hay
W: A pretty place for us to play?
W: So come with me, my sweet young thing,
W: I'll give to you my golden ring."
W:
W: 4. So he took off his ring of gold,
W: "Oh, pretty miss, do this behold,
W: Freely will I give it for your maidenhead."
W: Her cheeks they blushed like the roses red.
W:
W: 5. "Come give that ring into my hand,
W: And I will neither stay nor stand;
W: For your ring it is worth much more to me,
W: Than twenty maidenheads," said she.
W:
W: 6. But as he made for the pooks of hay,
W: She leapt on his horse and she tore away.
W: He called and he called, but he called in vain,
W: For Joan she ne'er looked back again.
W:
W: 7. Nor did she think herself quite safe,
W: Not til she come to her true love's gate;
W: She'd robbed him of his horse and ring,
W: And left him to rage in the meadows green.
%
