%
W: 1. I am a maid that’s deep in love but yes I can complain,
W: I have in this world but one true love and Jimmy is his name.
W: And if I do not find my love I’ll mourn most constantly.
W: And I’ll find and follow Jimmy to the lands of liberty.
W:
W: 2. Then I’ll cut off my yellow locks, men’s clothing I’ll wear on.
W: I’ll sign to a bold sea captain, my passage I’ll work free.
W: And I’ll find and follow Jimmy to the lands of liberty.
W: (No 4th line)
W:
W: 3. One night all on the raging seas as we were going to bed,
W: The captain cried, “Farewell my boy, I wish you were a maid.
W: Your rosy cheeks and ruby lips they are enticing me,
W: And I wish to God with all my heart, a maid you were to me.”
W:
W: 4. “Then hold your tongue, dear Captain, such talk is all in vain,
W: And if the sailors find it out, they’d laugh and make much game.
W: For when we reach Columbia shore some prettier girls you’ll find
W: And you’ll laugh and sing and court with them, for courting you are inclined.”
W:
W: 5. Well it was no three days after our ship it reached the shore.
W: “Bid adieu, my loving Captain, adieu for ever more.
W: For once I was a sailor on sea but now I am a maid on shore,
W: So I bid adieu to you and all your crew, with you I’ll sail no more.”
W:
W: 6. “Come back, come back, my own pretty maid, come back and marry me,
W: I have three thousand pounds in gold and that I’ll give to thee.
W: So come back, come back, my own pretty maid, come back and marry me!”
W: (No 4th line)
%
