W: 1. Well, I've got a little lass and her name is Mary Mack,
W: Well make no mistake, she's the one I'm going to take.
W: There's a lot of other fellows would get up upon her track,
W: But I'm thinkin' that they'll have to get up early.
W:
W: Chorus:
W: Oh, Mary Mack's mother's making Mary Mack marry me,
W: My mother's making me marry Mary Mack.
W: I'm going to marry Mary to get married and take care of me.
W: We'll all be making merry when I marry Mary Mack.
W: Dee ump dum diddly idle deadle didle dum!
W:
W: 2. Well, this little lass, she's got a got of cash.
W: She's got a lot of brass; her father thinks I'm class.
W: I'd be a silly ass to let the matter pass;
W: Her father thinks I suit her very fairly.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 3. Well, Mary and her mother go an awful lot together,
W: In fact you never see the one, nor one without the other.
W: And all the fellows wonder if it's Mary or her mother,
W: Or both of them together that I'm courting.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 4. Well the wedding day's on Wednesday, and everything's arranged.
W: Her name will soon be changed to mine, unless her mind is changed.
W: I'm making the arrangements; I'm just about deranged,
W: For marriage is an awful undertaking.
W: (Chorus)
