w: Late-ly last night, I was asked to a wed_ding, The wed-ding of a fair maid who
A>A "G" G F | "E" E3 C/D/ | "A" E2 F>C | "G" D D E>E | "A" A, A, C E | "G" D B, "D" A,2 |
w: proved to me un-kind. For that day as she thought of her in-tend-ed young_ lo_ver,
"A" A2 "E" ^G>A | "A" E2 C>A, | "E" B,2 "D" A,>A, | A,4 |]
w: Thoughts of her old love had run through her mind.
%
W: 2. Supper being over and all things were ended,
W: Every young man was to sing a ﬁne song.
W: Until it came to the turn of her own foreign lover,
W: And the song that he sang, to the bride did belong.
W:
W: 3. How can you sit at another man’s table?
W: How can you drink of another man’s wine?
W: How can you lie in the arms of another?
W: Many’s the night, love, that you lay in mine.
W:
W: 4. Many the one has been seven years parted,
W: Seven years parted and did return again,
W: But I have only been two years away love,
W: Two years away love and did return again.
W:
W: 5. Sobbing and sighing, she rose from the table,
W: Sobbing and sighing, she went to her bed.
W: And early net morning when the bridgroom awakened,
W: He turned to embrace her and found she was dead.
W:
W: 6. Saying, "Annie, dear Annie, I knew you never loved me
W: My love and your love could never agree.
W: For I knew all along that your poor heart was breaking.
W: All for the sake of a foreign young man.
W:
W: 7. So now I must wear a frock of deep mourning.
W: A frock of deep mourning, one, two, and three.
W: I must wear to her wake my own wedding garment.
W: Ne’er again shall I go between the bark and the tree.
%
