w: A|North Coun-try maid up to|Lon- don had strayed, Al_|though with her na- ture it|did not a- gree, Which_|made her re- pent, and so|
"D" AG FE D2 EF |$ "Em" G2 FE "B" ^D2 EF | "Em" E2 (B^A) "B" B2 "^Chorus:" B3/2c/ || "G" d2 B3/2A/ "Em" G2 F3/2G/ |$
w: bit- ter- ly la- ment "Oh I|wish once a- gain for the|North Coun_try. Oh the|oak and the ash and the|
"Am" A/G3/2 FE "D" D2 (EF) | "Em" G2 FE "B" ^D2 EF | "Em" E2 "D" (E!courtesy!=D) "Em" E2 z2 |]
w: bon- nie i- vy tree, They_ |flour- ish at home in my|own coun_try!" |
%
W: 1. A North Country maid up to London had strayed,
W: Although with her nature it did not agree,
W: Which made her repent, and so bitterly lament,
W: Oh I wish once a gain for the North Country.
W:
W: Chorus:
W: Oh the oak and the ash and the bonnie ivy tree,
W: They flourish at home in my own country.
W:
W: 2. O fain would I be in the North Country,
W: Where the lads and lasses are making of hay;
W: There should I see what is pleasant to me,
W: A mischief light on them entic'd me away!
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 3. I like not the court, nor the city resort,
W: Since there is no fancy tor sucn maids as me;
W: Their pomp and their pride I can never abide,
W: Because with my humor it does not agree.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 4. How oft have I been in the Westmoreland green,
W: Where the young men and maidens resort for to play,
W: Where we with delight, from morning till night,
W: Could feast it and frolic on each holiday.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 5. The ewes and their lambs, with the kids and their dams:
W: To see in the country how finely they play;
W: The bells they do ring, and the birds they do sing,
W: And the fields and the gardens are pleasant and gay.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 6. At wakes and at fairs, being freed of all cares,
W: We there with our lovers did use for to dance;
W: Then hard hap had I, my ill fortune to try,
W: And so up to London, my steps to advance.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 7. But still I perceive, I a husband might have,
W: If I to the city my mind could but frame;
W: But I'll have a lad that is North Country bred,
W: Or else I'll not marry, in the mind that I am.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 8. A maiden I am, and a maid I'll remain,
W: Until my own country again do I see,
W: For here in this place I shall ne'er see the face
W: Of him that's allotted my love for to be.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 9. Then farewell my daddy, and farewell my mammy,
W: Until I do see you, I nothing but mourn;
W: Rememb'ring my brothers, my sisters, and others,
W: In less than a year I hope to return.
W: (Chorus)
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_fcae7ffd.abc

