W: 1. Oh, come, come with me to the old church yard,
W: I well know the path through the soft greensward,
W: Friends slumber there we were wont to regard,
W: We'll trace out their names in the old church yard.
W: Oh, mourn not for them, their grief is o’er,
W: Weep not for them, they weep no more,
W: For deep is their sleep though cold and hard.
W: Their pillows may be in the old church yard.
W:
W: 2. I know it seems vain when friends depart,
W: To breathe kind words to the broken heart,
W: I that the joys of life seem marred,
W: When we follow our friends to the old church yard.
W: But were I at rest beneath yon tree,
W: Why should you weep, dear friends for me?
W: I'm wayworn and sad, so why then retard
W: The rest that I seek in the old church yard?
W:
W: 3. Oh, weep not for me, I am anxious to go
W: To that haven of rest where tears never ﬂow.
W: I fear not to enter that dark lonely ward,
W: For soon I shall rise from the old church yard.
W: Yes, soon shall I join that heavenly band
W: Of glory-bound souls at my Savior’s right hand,
W: Forever to dwell in mansions prepared
W: For saints who shall rise from the old church yard.
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_9472f448.abc

