%
W: 1. A trooper lad cam' here ae nicht,
W: And oh, but he was weary;
W: A trooper lad came here ae nicht,
W: When the moon was shining clearly.
W:
W: Chorus:
W: Bonnie lassie, will ye lie near me,
W: Bonnie lassie, will ye lie near me?
W: An' I'll har a'your ribbons reel,
W: In the morn ere I leave ye.
W:
W: 2. She's ta'en the horse by the halter right
W: And led it to the stable;
W: She's gi'en him oats and hay to eat,
W: As muckle as he was able.
W:
W: 3. She's ta'en the sodger by the lily-white hand,
W: And led him to her chamber;
W: She's gi'en him a stoup o'wine to drink,
W: His love it fleered like aimber.
W:
W: 4. She's made her bed baith lang and wide,
W: She's made it like a lady;
W: She's ta'en her wee coatie over her heid,
W: Said, "Sodger, are ye ready?"
W:
W: 5. And he's ta'en off his belted coat,
W: Likewise his hat and feather,
W: And leaned his sword against the door,
W: And noo he's doon aside her.
W:
W: 6. They hadna been but an hour in bed,
W: An hour but and a quarter,
W: When the drum cam' soundin' up the street
W: And ilka feat was shorter.
W:
W: 7. "It's up, up, up, and our colonel cries,
W: It's up, up, up and away then;
W: I maun sheathe my sword in its scabbard case,
W: For tomorrow's our battle day then."
W:
W: 8. "And when will ye come back again,
W: My ain dear sodger laddie?
W: When will ye come back again,
W: And be your bairn's daddie?"
W:
W: 9. "O, haud your tongue, my bonnie wee lass,
W: Divna let this pairtin' grieve ye;
W: When heather coves grow ousen bows,
W: Bonnie lassie, I'll come and see ye."
W:
W: 10. She's ta'en her wee coatie over her heid,
W: And followed him up to Stirlin',
W: She's grown sae fu' that she couldna boo,
W: And he's left her in Dunfermline.
W:
W: 11. It's breid and cheese for carles and dames,
W: And oats and hay for horses;
W: A cup of tea for auld maids,
W: And bonnie lads for lasses.
%
%
% Source: sources/dmc-lizmilner-com/pages/viewabc.vbhtml_9ef164e5.abc

