W: 2. "In behind yon auld fail dyke,
W: I wot there lies a new-slain knight;
W: And naebody kens that he lies there, O
W: But his hawk and his hound and his lady fair, O,
W: His hawk and his hound and his lady fair."
W:
W: 3. "His hound is tae the hunting gane,
W: His hawk tae fetch the wildfowl hame;
W: His lady's ta'en anither mate, O.
W: So we may mak our dinner sweet, O,
W: We may mak our dinner sweet."
W:
W: 4. "Ye'll sit on his white hause-bane
W: And I'll pike out his bonny blue een;
W: Wi' many a lock of his golden hair, O,
W: We'll theek our nest when it grows bare, O,
W: Theek our nest when it grows bare."
W:
W: 5. "Many a one for him maks mane,
W: But nane shall ken where he is gane;
W: O'er his white bones when they are bare, O.
W: The wind shall blow for evermair, O,
W: The wind shall blow for evermair."
