W:
W: 2. Oh, me and four went poaching, out in Squire Vernon's Park
W: We were thinking for to catch some game, the weather being dark.
W: But to our great misfortune, we were all caught with speed,
W: And they sent us off to Warwick gaol which made our hearts to bleed.
W:
W: 3. And at Warwick assizes at the bar we did appear
W: And like Job, we stood with patience our sentence for to hear.
W: But being old offenders, it made our case go hard,
W: And for twenty cold and cruel years, we were all forced on board.
W:
W: 4. Oh, we had a female comrade, Sue Sommers was her name,
W: And she also was taken, for the selling of our game.
W: But the Captain fell in love with her, and he married her out of hand,
W: And she was kind and true to us, going to Van Diemen's Land.
W:
W: 5. As I lay on the deck last night, a-dreaming of my home,
W: I dreamt I was in Aylesbury, the fields and woods among.
W: With my true love beside me and a jug of ale in hand,
W: But I woke up broken hearted, going to Van Diemen's Land.
W:
W: 6. So, come all you gallant poachers, give heed unto my song,
W: It is a bit of good advice, all though it be not long.
W: Lay by your dog and snare, to you I do speak plain:
W: If you knew the hardships we endure, you'd never poach again.
%
