W:2. "What ails you, what ails you, my daughter Janet
W:That you look so pale and wan
W:Have you had any sore sickness
W:Or yet been sleeping with a man?"
W:
W:3. "I have not had any sore sickness
W:Nor yet been sleeping with a man
W:It is for you, my father dear,
W:For biding so long in Spain"
W:
W:4. "Cast off, cast off your berry-brown gown
W:Stand naked on the stone
W:That I may know you by your shape
W:If you be a maiden or none"
W:
W:5. So she cast off her berry-brown gown
W:Stood naked on the stone
W:Her apron was low, her haunches round
W:Her face was pale and wan
W:
W:6. "Was it with a lord or a duke or a knight
W:Or a man of birth and fame
W:Or was it with one of my serving men
W:That's lately come out of Spain?"
W:
W:7. "No it wasn't with a lord or a duke or a knight
W:Nor a man of birth and fame
W:But it was with Willie of Winsbury
W:I could bide no longer alone"
W:
W:8. The king has called on his merry men all
W:By thirty and by three
W:Saying "Fetch me this Willie of Winsbury
W:For hanged he shall be"
W:
W:9. But when he came the king before
W:He was clad all in the red silk
W:His hair was like the strands of gold
W:His skin was as white as the milk
W:
W:10. "And it is no wonder," said the king
W:"That my daughter's love you did win
W:For if I was a woman, as I am a man
W:My bedfellow you would have been"
W:
W:11. "Now will you marry my daughter Janet
W:By the truth of your right hand?
W:Oh will you marry my daughter Janet
W:I'll make you lord of my land"
W:
W:12. "Well yes, I'll marry your daughter Janet
W:By the truth of my right hand
W:Well yes I'll marry your daughter Janet
W:But I'll not be the lord of your land"
W:
W:13. He's mounted her on a milk-white steed
W:Himself on a dapple grey
W:He has made her the lady of as much land
W:As she shall ride in a long summer's day
